+ <$osa_media_flash,scale=1.5>\nManage Git Branches\nand Workflows

++ Trunk-based Development
+++ Definition
++++_ Single trunk/master/mainline branch
++++_ Everyone clones the branch, makes changes and pushes directly to the main branch
++++_ No branches, no pull requests
++++_ Simple branching strategy
+++_ True enabler of/strongly associated with
++++ Continuous Deployment
+++ Requires
++++ High team maturity
++++ Feature Flags
+++++_ critical
++++++_ frequent merges
++++++_ half baked features not visible to users
++++_ Quality automated tests
+++++ TDD
+++++ DDD
+++_ Suitable if...
++++_ Mature team that understand the importance of automation and maintains thorough test coverage.
++++_ You don't care about quality
+++_ **Madness Level**
++++ Depends
+++++_ on how it is used and who is using it

++ Feature Branches\n(GitHub Workflow)

+++ Definition
++++_ Similar to trunk-based but... create features (small chunks)
++++_ Small delivery cycles between a couple hours to a couple of days
++++_ Create
+++++ PR
++++++_ code reviews
++++++_ Sent to QA Testing
++++++_ If QA passes, **merge** to main
+++_ True enabler of
++++ Continous Integration
+++++_ and if applied correctly
++++++ Continuous Delivery
++++_ speed up software change\nand maintain quality
++++_ with
+++++_ Azure CLI
+++++_ Azure DevOps CLI
+++++_ Integrate with
++++++_ Git and Agile tools

+++_ Very useful to have
++++ Feature Flags
+++++_ for long running features

+++ Main Branch
++++_ Always
+++++ Deployable
++++++_ No broken code\nalways functional
++++_ Protected by
+++++ Policies
++++++_ Pull Requests
++++++_ Code Reviews
++++++_ Successful Builds
++++++_ Passing Unit Tests

+++_ used for
++++ New Features
++++ Bug Fixes
++++ Hot Fixes
+++_ Suitable for...
++++_ Small self-sufficient teams, small code bases and you know how to split work so that it can be done fast
++++_ Microservices and distributed high-scale cloud architectures
++++_ Blue/Green, A/B and Canary deployments
+++_ **Madness Level**
++++ Low

++ Fork Branching
+++ Definition
++++_ Fork whole repository
++++_ Work on a feature and create a PR back to the upstream
+++ Advantage
++++_ Do not need to have access to the original repository
++++_ Yours when you fork it
+++_ Suitable for...
++++_ Open-Source, Organizational Inner-Source, and very large teams
+++_ **Madness Level**
++++ Low

-- Release Branching
---_ Longer lasting branches (weeks or months)
---_ Different teams may be working on different release branches
---_ Complications occur with hot fixes and bug fixes
---_ Anything merged to the main branch needs to be applied on every other release branch
---_ There is no continuous anything...
---_ Low frequency deployments
---_ Single word to associate with this branching strategy is **Waterfall (faking Agile)**
---_ Run away, unless your solutions absolutely MUST support multiple versions
---_ Might need a dedicated person to branch, validate, and merge.
---_ **Madness Level**
---- Medium
---_ Suitable if...
----_ Need to maintain backward compatibility and apply hot fixes to multiple releases

-- Git Flow Branching
---_ From the main branch create a dev branch and then off dev create Feature-1, Feature-2 etc. Create release branches off the dev branch.
---_ Everything needs to be merged, ultimately, to the main branch. Hot fixes and bugs are an absolute pain.
---_ Requires a dedicated full time release manager
---_ Get rid of it
----_ **VERY BAD FOR CONTINUOUS INTEGRATION**
---_ **Madness Level**
---- High

-- Environment Branching
---_ A dev branch and then a branch for every environment (staging, qa, prod)
---_ Also have release branches (release-1, release-2, release-3...) merges multiplied by the no. of releases in paralllel
---_ Hot fixes and bug fixes are a nightmare.
---_ The concept is flawed, as a release can be deployed to different environments.
---_ Suitable...
----_ **NEVER**
---_ **Madness Level**
---- Insane

@endmindmap